M I Z A R   F O U N D A T I O N S
============================================================
511.3224 Large cardinals & universes

The file "Mizar Foundations" outlines the foundational choices and 
style of the Mizar formalization framework: a ZF-style set theory 
extended with the Tarski–Grothendieck universe, primitive tuple/pair/
relation constructions, and a soft-typing, set‑based style where 
mathematical objects are sets up to extensional equality.

The Tarski–Grothendieck axiom is labeled “stronger” only because
it assumes the existence of very large infinite universes. This
does not imply superiority; it simply reflects the classical
classification of axioms by the infinities they posit.

[1] FOUNDATIONAL BASIS
------------------------------------------------------------
• Core Logic: ZF-style set theory
• Added Axiom: Tarski–Grothendieck Universe (TG)
• Strength: ≈ ZFC + inaccessible cardinal
• Everything is a set; equality is EXTENSIONAL.

  EXTENSIONAL EQUALITY:
      A = B  <=>  ∀x (x ∈ A ↔ x ∈ B)

[2] PRIMITIVE CONSTRUCTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------
• Tuples: primitive objects, not Kuratowski encodings
• Ordered pairs: primitive (TARSKI:def 2)
• Relations: primitive sets of ordered pairs

  Rich relational calculus:
      - domain
      - range
      - inverse
      - composition
      - relational image

This refer only to a technical notion used in classical set theory:
the existence of larger infinite objects.

    • not “more powerful” in a practical sense
    • not “more expressive” for everyday mathematics
    • not a philosophical ranking of foundations

[3] MIZAR STYLE
------------------------------------------------------------
• Set-theoretic universe
• Soft typing layer
• Structures built on extensional equality
• Mathematical objects = sets with structure

[4] DRAWBACKS AND REASONS NOT TO USE MIZAR FOUNDATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------
• SET-ONLY ONTOLOGY
    Everything is a set. No native type theory, no higher-order
    primitives. Users wanting inductive types, dependent types,
    or polymorphism must encode them manually as set-structures.

• SOFT TYPING LIMITATIONS
    Typing is predicate-based and non-coercive. Type errors often
    manifest as failed proofs rather than structural mismatches.
    Complex structures require many auxiliary registrations.

• FIRST-ORDER ONLY
    No higher-order logic. Quantifying over predicates, functions,
    or classes requires encodings. This can make some modern
    mathematics (especially category theory or HoTT-style work)
    more verbose than in HOL/Coq/Lean.

• TG AXIOM STRENGTH
    Tarski–Grothendieck implies inaccessible cardinals. Some users
    prefer weaker foundations (pure ZFC, NBG, ETCS, HoTT) and may
    view TG as stronger than necessary for everyday mathematics.

• PRIMITIVE TUPLES / RELATIONS
    While convenient, these primitives differ from standard
    Kuratowski encodings. Porting proofs to other systems often
    requires translation layers or re-encoding of tuples/relations.

• LEGACY INFRASTRUCTURE
    Mizar’s language and tooling are mature but older. Integration
    with modern proof ecosystems (Lean, Coq, Isabelle) is limited.
    Automation is intentionally conservative, requiring more manual
    guidance compared to newer systems.

• CATEGORY-THEORETIC FRICTION
    Large categories are “small” only relative to a TG universe.
    Users who prefer explicit size distinctions (small/large/locally
    small) may find Mizar’s universe-based approach less transparent.

• STEEP LEARNING CURVE
    The combination of soft typing, strict syntax, and conservative
    automation can be challenging for newcomers. Proofs often require
    detailed justification steps that other systems automate.

[5] THE “FALLACY OF INFINITIES”
------------------------------------------------------------
The Tarski–Grothendieck axiom postulates the existence of infinite 
universes, each of which corresponds to aninaccessible cardinal. This 
commits the system to extremely large infinities without mathematical
necessity.

Core objections:
    • UNNECESSARY 
        Most mathematics does not require inaccessible cardinals. 
        TG introduces far more set-theoretic power than typical 
        ZFC developments.

    • SIZE-OBSCURING
        Treating large categories as “small relative to a
        universe” hides genuine size distinctions. Some
        category-theoretic arguments become less transparent.

    • FOUNDATIONAL INFLATION
        TG inflates ontology: instead of proving existence
        of large structures, the axiom grants them wholesale.

        An axiom is called stronger when it asserts the existence
        of more expansive infinite sets (e.g., inaccessible cardinals).
        This is a statement about consistency strength, not usefulness.

        An axiom is called weaker when it commits to fewer or smaller
        infinite sets. It does not imply inferiority or practical limits;
        it simply avoids assuming certain large infinities.

    • PHILOSOPHICAL SKEPTICISM
        Grothendieck universes are seen by some as a
        convenience axiom rather than a principled foundation.
        Assuming huge sets exist simply because they make 
        category theory tidy.

    • PORTABILITY COST
        Formalizations relying on TG may not translate
        directly to weaker systems (ZFC, NBG, ETCS, HoTT),
        requiring re‑engineering of size arguments.
